<Project>

  <!--
    Every shipped package under src/. Nothing else in the repo inherits this - tests, Playground,
    bench and Research sit outside it on purpose.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Generating the XML means the compiler finally PARSES the doc comments. Without it Roslyn
      skips them entirely, which is how roughly eleven <summary> blocks drifted onto neighbouring
      members across four independent areas without anything complaining - including a warning
      about a bad MAC looking like a quiet peer, stranded on the wrong method.

      It also ships the docs with the packages, so the prose these files are full of reaches
      IntelliSense instead of stopping at the repo.
    -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>

    <!--
      CS1591 is "missing XML comment for publicly visible member". Not every public member is
      documented and that is a deliberate choice, not a defect - the comments here explain what is
      surprising, not what is obvious. Suppressed so the warnings that DO matter stay visible:
      CS1570 malformed XML, CS1571 duplicate tag, CS1572 param that does not exist, CS1574 broken
      cref.

      CS1573 goes with it, for the same reason one step down: it fires when SOME parameters are
      documented and others are not, which is the house style rather than a defect. A member
      explains the one argument that surprises people and leaves `reactor` and `keyPemPath` alone.
      Silencing it is cheaper than padding every signature back out with tags that say nothing.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591;CS1573</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
